  "narrative": "The new evidence sharply separates exploratory hypotheses from the robust large effects. For the lower-prior exploratory claims, the new estimates are near zero and do not establish the hypothesized associations at the 10% relative clinical-significance benchmark. H13 (MYC amplification with stronger KIF18A dependency), H14 (ERBB2 amplification with stronger STAT3 dependency), and H15 (EGFR amplification with stronger TMED10 dependency) have signed point estimates below the approximate 10% threshold for their outcomes, and their 95% intervals include null and some clinically significant values; therefore they remain unresolved but are deferred rather than prioritized. H16 (KRAS mutation with greater RIT1 dependency) has an upper bound below the approximate 10% threshold and very small point estimate, so it is rejected as unsupported in this dataset.\n\nThe four accepted large effects now have much stronger robustness support. The SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 effect is very large within adenocarcinoma (H19: signed 0.777, interval 0.755 to 0.799) and in high-quality screens with screen_roc_auc >=0.95 (H31: signed 0.790, interval 0.765 to 0.816). The TP53-loss USP7 effect is very large within adenocarcinoma (H24: signed 0.385, interval 0.299 to 0.471) and in high-quality screens (H32: signed 0.426, interval 0.340 to 0.513). The MSI-high WRN effect is very large within adenocarcinoma (H25: signed 0.784, interval 0.748 to 0.820) and in high-quality screens (H33: signed 0.795, interval 0.758 to 0.832). The reversed KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 effect is also very large within adenocarcinoma (H29: signed 0.793, interval 0.769 to 0.817) and in high-quality screens (H34: signed 0.790, interval 0.763 to 0.816). These intervals are far from null and far above any reasonable 10% relative threshold, so I accept these refinements as dataset-specific associations and close them as completed robustness checks.\n\nI choose to request independent validation for H22 rather than for a refinement because H22 is the most biologically surprising accepted claim: KEAP1 loss was associated with weaker rather than stronger NFE2L2 dependency. Validation of the original reversed claim is therefore most valuable for guarding against a discovery artifact. The accepted effects remain limited to this supplied lung cancer cell-line dataset; they are not causal and not yet established as general biological relationships.",
  "research_notes": "Updated evidence map: Accepted dataset-specific claims now include original H5 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency, H8 TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency, H9 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency, and H22 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency. Refinements H19, H24, H25, H29 (adenocarcinoma strata) and H31, H32, H33, H34 (high-quality screen strata) are accepted and closed, confirming that the large effects persist in the dominant histologic subtype and higher-confidence screens.\n\nNewly assessed exploratory results: H13 KIF18A/MYC, H14 STAT3/ERBB2 amplification, and H15 TMED10/EGFR amplification remain unresolved but deferred because point estimates are small and intervals are inconclusive. H16 RIT1/KRAS is rejected because the upper 95% bound is below the approximate 10% threshold.\n\nValidation request: H22 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency, because it is both the largest and the direction-reversing accepted effect; independent validation is useful to guard against a discovery artifact.\n\nNext priorities: (1) prioritize registered specificity checks H43-H47: ARID1A loss for SMARCA2 after excluding SMARCA4 loss, KEAP1/TP53 specificity checks for NFE2L2 and USP7, MSI-high WRN independent of TP53 loss, and KRAS mutation effects after excluding BRAF/EGFR/ERBB2 drivers. (2) Continue pending non-Avana library checks H35-H37 and interactions H27-H28 if useful. (3) Do not prioritize H3 EGFR unless H18 or H41 shows a clear larger subgroup effect. (4) At synthesize, assess H22 validation immediately and keep all conclusions constrained to associations in this dataset.",
